PR #223  overall: bug_confirmed
Spec: Without a dedicated SSE event ID for unknown NMIs, implementations may silently swallow unclassifiable NMIs, depriving supervisor software of critical hardware fault awareness
  [BUG] arch/riscv/include/asm/sbi.h:15-49  enum sbi_ext_id
      SBI_EXT_SSE (0x535345) is absent from the extension ID enumeration. Every other ratified SBI extension (TIME, IPI, RFENCE, HSM, SRST, PMU, DBCN, NACL, FWFT, MPXY, DBTR) is enumerated, but SSE is missing. Without this constant, sbi_probe_extension() cannot discover SSE, and no SSE ecalls can be routed to firmware. This is the root cause: the kernel literally has no token to identify the SSE extension.
  [BUG] arch/riscv/kernel/sbi.c:649-709  sbi_init
      sbi_init() probes and initializes TIME, IPI, RFENCE, SRST, DBCN, and FWFT extensions but contains no SSE probe. The function already checks sbi_spec_version >= sbi_mk_version(3, 0) for FWFT — demonstrating the kernel is aware of SBI v3.0 capabilities. An SSE probe guarded by the same version check is missing. Without it, the kernel never registers SSE event interest with firmware, so the SBI_SSE_EVENT_LOCAL_UNKNOWN_NMI channel is never established.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kernel/sbi.c:543-553  sbi_probe_extension
      sbi_probe_extension() is a generic, correct gateway. It issues SBI_EXT_BASE_PROBE_EXT via ecall for any extid and returns the value on success, 0 on error. The function itself has no bug — it would correctly probe SBI_EXT_SSE if called with that ID. The gap is upstream: no caller passes 0x535345.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/asm/sbi.h:460-480  enum sbi_mpxy_attribute_id
      SBI_MPXY_ATTR_SSE_EVENT_ID (0x00000006) defines the channel attribute slot for binding an SSE event to an MPXY channel, and SBI_MPXY_CHAN_CAP_SSE (BIT(1)) defines the capability bit. This establishes the structural precedent for SSE-event-aware channels. However, no actual SSE event ID constants (LOCAL_UNKNOWN_NMI = 0xffff0001, GLOBAL_UNKNOWN_NMI = 0xffff0002) are defined anywhere in sbi.h. The attribute slot exists but has no valid values to store. Without these definitions, the MPXY channel attribute is an empty container.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/entry.S:247-318  ret_from_exception
      ret_from_exception is the sret/mret boundary where firmware-injected SSE events would be pending when returning from M-mode to S-mode. The code restores STATUS, EPC, and all GPRs before executing sret/mret. There is no check for pending SSE events and no interception point. If firmware injected an SSE event (e.g., LOCAL_UNKNOWN_NMI) that manifests as a pending interrupt or modified cause code on the next trap, the kernel would not specifically recognize it. Currently safe only because the kernel hasn't registered for SSE events — but if firmware independently injects SSE events, the kernel has no awareness.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/entry.S:125-238  handle_exception
      handle_exception is the unified trap entry point. At lines 218-238, exceptions are dispatched via excp_vect_table lookup. When the cause code lies outside the table bounds, control falls through to the do_trap_unknown fallback (line 236-237). This dispatch path would correctly route an SSE-injected event with an unknown cause code to do_trap_unknown, so the dispatch logic itself is sound. The risk is that the event arrives as a raw S-mode exception rather than through a dedicated SSE software channel — losing the context that this is a firmware-forwarded hardware error.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/entry.S:480-501  excp_vect_table
      The exception vector table explicitly dispatches reserved cause codes 16 and 17 to do_trap_unknown. These reserved slots correspond exactly to the class of conditions that firmware should forward via LOCAL_UNKNOWN_NMI. However, the dispatch is purely cause-code-driven: there is no distinction between 'hardware generated a reserved cause' and 'firmware injected an SSE event with an unknown cause.' Both land in do_trap_unknown. The table correctly routes unknown causes to the fallback handler, but the handler lacks hardware-error semantics.
  [BUG] arch/riscv/kernel/traps.c:166-167  do_trap_unknown
      do_trap_unknown is instantiated via DO_ERROR_INFO with SIGILL/ILL_ILLTRP semantics — 'illegal instruction, illegal trap.' This is the actual symptom of the vulnerability: an unclassifiable hardware fault (e.g., uncorrectable ECC error, machine-check exception, hardware integrity failure) that firmware should have forwarded via LOCAL_UNKNOWN_NMI instead reaches S-mode as a raw trap and is reported as an illegal instruction. The kernel loses all hardware-error context: no error record, no machine-check architecture integration, no RAS (Reliability/Availability/Serviceability) event logging. A hardware integrity failure is indistinguishable from a corrupted instruction stream.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/asm/csr.h:105-123  (exception cause defines)
      The exception cause code definitions jump from EXC_STORE_PAGE_FAULT (15) to EXC_INST_GUEST_PAGE_FAULT (20), leaving causes 16-19 undefined. Causes 16 and 17 are explicitly marked as do_trap_unknown in excp_vect_table. The RISC-V privileged spec reserves these cause codes, but hardware implementations may generate them for implementation-specific error conditions. Without SSE LOCAL_UNKNOWN_NMI forwarding, these hardware-generated reserved-cause exceptions arrive at do_trap_unknown as SIGILL. This gap is the hardware-side trigger for the vulnerability.
  [OK] drivers/mailbox/riscv-sbi-mpxy-mbox.c:47-68  struct sbi_mpxy_channel_attrs
      The sbi_mpxy_channel_attrs struct correctly includes an sse_event_id field at line 61, aligned with the SBI_MPXY_ATTR_SSE_EVENT_ID attribute slot defined in sbi.h. The struct is read from firmware via mpxy_read_attrs(). If LOCAL_UNKNOWN_NMI (0xffff0001) were defined as an SSE event ID constant, channels could be bound to it. The struct definition is correct; the gap is the missing event ID constant definitions and the absence of any code that registers interest in SSE events through MPXY channels.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kernel/traps.c:132-164  DO_ERROR_INFO / do_trap_error
      The DO_ERROR_INFO macro and do_trap_error() provide the correct pattern for exception handlers: irqentry_nmi_enter/exit in kernel mode, irqentry_enter_from_user_mode/exit_to_user_mode for user mode. do_trap_error() correctly records bad_cause, attempts fixup_exception() for kernel-mode faults, and calls die() if unfixable. This is precisely the pattern a future SSE event handler (for LOCAL_UNKNOWN_NMI) would follow — the infrastructure for handling NMIs with proper context tracking already exists.

