Spec: Without a dedicated SSE event ID for unknown NMIs, implementations may silently swallow unclassifiable NMIs, depriving supervisor software of critical hardware fault awareness
  [BUG] lib/sbi/sbi_trap.c:245-370  sbi_trap_nonaia_irq / sbi_trap_aia_irq / sbi_trap_handler
      Unrecognized M-mode interrupts return SBI_ENOENT from both sbi_trap_nonaia_irq (L250) and sbi_trap_aia_irq (L281). This non-zero rc propagates to trap_done (L368-370), which calls sbi_trap_error() -> sbi_hart_hang(). An unknown NMI (e.g., uncorrectable ECC error, machine-check exception) arriving at M-mode causes the hart to hang instead of being forwarded to S-mode via SBI_SSE_EVENT_LOCAL_UNKNOWN_NMI. The spec requires forwarding via 0xffff0001; the code silently kills the hart. This is a direct violation with function_loss consequence: the OS supervisor never learns about the hardware error condition.
  [RISK] include/sbi/sbi_ecall_interface.h:446-449  (event ID definitions)
      SBI_SSE_EVENT_LOCAL_UNKNOWN_NMI (0xffff0001) is not defined. LOCAL_RESERVED_3_START is currently 0xffff0001, which is exactly the ID the spec mandates for unknown NMI. Adding the definition requires consuming this slot and bumping LOCAL_RESERVED_3_START to 0xffff0002. The absence is a prerequisite gap: even if trap-handler code were patched, there is no event ID constant to reference.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_sse.c:198-207  standard_events[] / sse_is_standard_event
      standard_events[] array lists all standard SSE event IDs but has no entry for UNKNOWN_NMI. Once the event ID is defined, it must be added here so sse_is_standard_event() returns true for it, which is required to correctly return SBI_ERR_NOT_SUPPORTED on REGISTER/ENABLE calls for standard events (vs SBI_ERR_INVALID_PARAM for platform events). Missing this entry would cause the SSE registration flow to misclassify the event.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_double_trap.c:17-37  sbi_double_trap_handler / sbi_double_trap_init
      Correct architectural template. sbi_double_trap_handler (L17-31) checks prev_mode==PRV_S, handles VS-mode redirect via sbi_trap_redirect(), and injects via sbi_sse_inject_event(). sbi_double_trap_init (L33-37) conditionally registers the event via sbi_sse_add_event() gated on SBI_HART_EXT_SSDBLTRP. This is the exact pattern a new unknown-NMI handler should follow. Code itself is bug-free.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_sse.c:944-947  sbi_sse_inject_event
      Simple one-line wrapper calling sse_inject_event(event_id, current_hartid()). This is the API the unknown-NMI handler would call. Infrastructure is correct — no logic defect. The function itself is not the gap; the gap is that nothing calls it for unknown NMIs.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_sse.c:949-969  sbi_sse_add_event
