  [OK] include/sbi/sbi_sse.h:62-69  (public API declarations)
      Correct declarations for sbi_sse_add_event() and sbi_sse_inject_event(). No logic present — these are just function prototypes. A new unknown-NMI subsystem would include this header and call both APIs.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_init.c:293-354  init_coldboot / init_warm_startup
      Cold boot path (L293-354) has the correct insertion window between sbi_double_trap_init() and sbi_sse_init(). However the warm boot path init_warm_startup (L407-481) does NOT call sbi_double_trap_init() nor any equivalent, and its sbi_sse_init() call is at L465. If an unknown-NMI init call is only added to the cold boot path, warm-booted harts will lack the registration. This invariant — that warm boot must also register — is not enforced in code and depends on the developer remembering to add it in both paths.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_sse.c:181-192  supported_events / static event info structs
      Defines the static SBI_SLIST_HEAD (supported_events) and two statically-defined event info structs (global_software_event, local_software_event) pre-linked into the list. A new UNKNOWN_NMI event could be added either as a static struct (like software events) or dynamically via sbi_sse_add_event() (like PMU/double-trap). Both patterns are available and correct. The data structures themselves have no defect.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_pmu.c:338-346  sbi_pmu_ovf_irq
