PR #223  overall: bug_confirmed
Spec: Without a dedicated SSE event ID for unknown NMIs, implementations may silently swallow unclassifiable NMIs, depriving supervisor software of critical hardware fault awareness
  [BUG] sys/riscv/riscv/intc.c:276-279  intc_intr
      Any interrupt with scause code >= INTC_NIRQS (16) is silently dropped by returning FILTER_HANDLED — no logging, no panic, no counter increment, no SSE event forward. S-mode standard interrupt codes only go to 15, but if firmware uses a non-standard scause interrupt code >= 16 to signal an NMI-like event, it vanishes entirely at this boundary. This is the exact vulnerability condition described in the spec: unclassifiable interrupt arrives at S-mode and disappears without trace. The return value FILTER_HANDLED specifically tells the interrupt framework the interrupt was consumed, preventing any fallback processing.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/riscv/trap.c:372-375  do_trap_supervisor
      Unknown non-interrupt exception codes fall through to a panic via the default case. If firmware maps an unclassifiable NMI to a reserved scause exception code (bit 63 clear, code 24-31 reserved for custom use), the kernel hard-crashes the system. While panicking on truly unknown exceptions is a defensible hardening choice, it converts a hardware-notification event into a complete system outage — the opposite of 'forward and notify gracefully'. No attempt is made to log the event as a machine-check or hardware-error type before panicking. The panic path also prevents any SSE event injection back to firmware since the kernel is dead.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/riscv/trap.c:455-458  do_trap_user
      Same panic-on-unknown-exception pattern as do_trap_supervisor, applied to user-mode traps. If firmware routes an NMI event through a user-mode scause exception code (e.g. a reserved code 24-31 with bit 63 clear, arriving while SSTATUS_SPP=0), the kernel panics. The assumption that all user-mode exceptions are caused by the current user process breaks when firmware injects hardware error notifications through this channel. The kernel panics rather than forwarding the event or delivering a signal to a monitoring daemon.
  [BUG] sys/riscv/include/sbi.h:68-113  
      Defines SBI extension ID constants for TIME (0x54494D45), IPI (0x735049), RFNC (0x52464E43), HSM (0x48534D), and SRST (0x53525354) but completely lacks SBI_EXT_ID_SSE (0x535345). This is the structural root cause: without this constant, the kernel cannot probe, register for, or receive SSE events from firmware. The SBI spec v3.0 defines SSE as the standard channel for firmware-to-OS notification of hardware events including SBI_SSE_EVENT_LOCAL_UNKNOWN_NMI (0xffff0001). This file is the single enumeration point for all SBI extensions the kernel knows about — the absence of SSE here means the kernel has zero SSE protocol awareness at any layer.
  [BUG] sys/riscv/riscv/sbi.c:314-322  sbi_init
      SBI init probes only TIME, IPI, RFNC, and SRST extensions. No SSE probe is performed. The kernel never calls sbi_probe_extension(SBI_EXT_ID_SSE) — even if the hypothetical constant were added — so it never discovers whether firmware supports SSE event forwarding. This is the active-side counterpart to the missing constant in sbi.h: even a firmware that implements SBI v3.0 SSE events with SBI_SSE_EVENT_LOCAL_UNKNOWN_NMI would never be discovered by FreeBSD. The kernel also never registers any SSE event handler with firmware, so firmware has no destination for forwarded NMI events.Wrote 3 report(s) → output/riscv-sbi-doc/pr-223/

  [RISK] sys/riscv/riscv/exception.S:219-225  cpu_exception_handler_supervisor
      The S-mode exception entry point saves/restores supervisor registers via save_registers/load_registers and returns via sret. There is no mnret path and no handler registered for the mncause vector (Smrnmi extension). The use of sret is architecturally correct for standard S-mode traps, but the kernel has no code path for receiving M-mode NMIs via the RNMI delegation mechanism. If hardware implements Smrnmi and firmware delegates NMI handling to S-mode, the CPU would vector through mncause and find no handler — resulting in undefined behavior or an M-mode fallback that may drop the event. The entry point's structure (saving only S-mode GPRs and sstatus) would be insufficient for NMI context even if a handler were wired, since NMI save/restore requires mstatus/mepc/mcause context.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/riscv/plic.c:158-168  plic_irq_dispatch
      PLIC IRQ dispatch treats every interrupt number as a regular device interrupt via intr_isrc_dispatch(). There is no NMI-aware classification, no special handling for reserved interrupt numbers, and no SSE event forwarding path. If firmware routes an NMI event through a reserved PLIC interrupt number, the hardware NMI semantics (non-maskable, highest priority, requires special acknowledgement) are completely lost — the event is demoted to a regular maskable interrupt. Unknown IRQs that fail dispatch produce a 'Stray irq' printf but no event is forwarded to any monitoring subsystem or security event channel.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/riscv/aplic.c:222-246  aplic_intr
      The APLIC (AIA advanced interrupt controller) handler has the identical pattern to PLIC — a claimi loop that dispatches all interrupts through aplic_irq_dispatch() with no NMI classification and no SSE event forwarding. The handler reads the claimi register, extracts IRQ number and priority, then passes them to the generic dispatch. Unknown IRQs are printed as stray only in bootverbose, making them even less visible than the PLIC path. The AIA specification supports message-signaled interrupts and has a richer event namespace than PLIC, increasing the surface area for non-standard NMI routing. No priority-based classification distinguishes NMI-level (highest priority) events from regular device interrupts.
  [OK] sys/riscv/riscv/trap.c:333-371  do_trap_supervisor
      The supervisor exception switch handles all standard RISC-V SCAUSE exception codes (0-15, 20-23) defined by the Privileged ISA specification. There are no standard 'machine check' (24) or 'hardware error' (25) scause codes in the ratified RISC-V privileged architecture — codes 24-31 are reserved for custom use. The switch is complete for the standardized exception code space. The default case panic is a deliberate design choice for true unknown exceptions. The finding description in the security spec incorrectly references SCAUSE_MACHINE_CHECK=24 and SCAUSE_HARDWARE_ERROR=25 as if they were standard encodings, but the FreeBSD riscvreg.h correctly reflects the ratified ISA encoding space.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/vmm/vmm_sbi.c:201-213  vmm_sbi_ecall
      The VMM SBI ecall handler forwards only RFNC, TIME, and IPI extensions to guests. Unknown extensions (including any hypothetical SSE) return false, causing the request to be bounced to userspace bhyve. This creates a multi-layered gap: even if the host kernel gained SSE support and could receive NMI events from firmware, guest VMs running under bhyve would never see them. The RISC-V hypervisor extension specification does define mechanisms for injecting NMI-type interrupts into guests (VM_INTINFO_NMI), but this code path never activates them because SSE events are not received at any layer.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/include/vmm.h:102-102  
